<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/6b7d8112b2f1678064f11dd472642b86f99ac373> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/parent> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/0b05ecb628329cf1ed5feb1b899a09450c7b0e41> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/7fcddbf6a518caccec163669c675aa52d77ac982> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/message> "Merge branch 'fix-flattening-none' into develop\n\n* fix-flattening-none:\n  Don't project non list elements in flattening operator" <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/4969648c326cd72a3ef8e497bd3d93b9982a2ca8> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/message> "Scalarize path expressions\n\nThe gist of this change is that:\n\nsearch('foo.bar', {'foo: {'bar': 'baz'}}) -> 'baz'\n\nPreviously search would always return a list of results,\nso the above example would return ['baz'] not 'baz'. While\nthis made it easy to explain (and implement), it never allowed\nyou to get a single scalar value, and as a result, relied on\nimplementations to provide an API to verify single result\nsearches.\n\nThere were a number of edge cases that change the semantics of what\nsearches return now that the results are scalarized, particularly\nwhen combined with wildcards.  See the .json files for specific\ncases, but high level, expressions with changed semantics:\n\n* `foo.*.baz[0]` returns the first sublist, not the first element\n  of each sublist.\n* `foo.*.*` now returns null.\n\nThe other major change is that when no matches were found, you'd\nget an empty list.  Now you get null (or in python, None)." <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
