<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/d6687aa215d2daaa41bcbf9928b90de106eb1b36> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/Commit> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/a4fd7c71f7ac4ad4e5784e7734546ba76396e3ef> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/parent> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/d4fa36a2dd242e2350a8992804730fef8164befd> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/8152eefe5281a9c2acbfb0d69275d00bee092727> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/Commit> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/196359a6f0147e41926efeaa5a93cacb3a385c7c> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/committedDate> "2016-12-06T21:15:58-08:00"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
<https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit/0835c1eba492d2e36c1ea47f6dfee2e6a92c1ef5> <https://repolex.ai/ontology/extracts/gitpython-developers/GitPython/v3.1.46/core/message> "Switch to hand written lexer/parser\n\nThis commit removes the dependency of PLY in favor of\na hand written lexer and parser.\n\nThe motivation for this was to fully support the new pipe syntax\n(JEP 4).  In order to do this we needed the high level feature of\nbeing able to stop projections when a '|' symbol was encountered.\n\nThis proved difficult because when a projection is needed in the\nprevious implemention, we'd create a `_Projection` node which contained\nthe specific semantics for handling projections.  It knew that when\nwe looked up a specific key that it internally needed to map that\nkey lookup onto each element in the projection.\n\nThis meant that in order to stop a projection we needed to find\nevery `_Projection` instance and convert it so a normal `list` type.\nThe only way to do this was to recursively crawl through the matched\nstructure and look for `_Projection` instances.\n\nA better way to do this is to restructure the AST nodes to represent\na projection via a `Projection` AST node.  This idea is shamelessly\nstolen from the PHP jmespath implementation.  For example, given the\nexpression `foo[*].bar.baz`, the previous AST matched the reference\ngrammar rule exactly and gave the AST of:\n\n```\nSubExpression(\n        SubExpression(\n                SubExpression(\n                        Field(foo),\n                        Index(*)),\n                Field(bar)),\n        Field(baz))\n```\n\nThe new AST is:\n\n```\nProjection(\n        Field(foo),\n        SubExpression(\n                Field(bar),\n                Field(baz)))\n```\n\nThis makes the AST for a pipe straightforward:\n\n```\nPipe(\n        Projection(\n                Field(foo),\n                SubExpression(\n                        Field(bar),\n                        Field(baz))),\n        IndexExpression(\n                Identity(),\n                Index(0)))\n```\n\nIn terms of implementation, the lexer is a regex based lexer.\nWe compile the list of regexes of each of the respective tokens\ninto a single master regex and use that for the lexing process.\n\nThe parser is a Pratt parser (top down operator precedence parser),\nand follows the original paper's semantics fairly closely.  While the\nparse module has more info on the algorithm there's a few points of\ninterest:\n\n* The new grammar that includes projection AST nodes cannot be parsed\n  with a single token of lookahead.  We need LL(2) in order to\n  distinguish multi-select-list and projections.  Specifically given a\n  `[` token, if we then see `*`, we don't know if we should parse that\n  as a multi-select list or as a projection (`[*]`).\n\n* Interestingly enough, the fastest implementation was to create a fixed\n  list of tokens and track the current index.  This also makes additional\n  tokens of lookahead really simple.  The previous implementation used\n  an iterator to look for the next token only when asked.  Even though\n  this new approach requires two passes through the input (one to create\n  the list of tokens, and through the tokens to create the AST), this\n  actually turns out to be the fastest implementation.\n\nPerformance is drastically improved switching to a handwritten lexer\nand parser.\n\nPreviously (@7480643df8ad0c99269b1fa6b9e793582bcb7efe):\n\n```\n$ python perf/perftest.py\nlex_time: 1.10602379, parse_time: 1.45006180ms, search_time: 0.00190735ms description: Basic minimal case name: single_expression\nlex_time: 1.11699104, parse_time: 1.37901306ms, search_time: 0.00190735ms description: Basic minimal case name: single_dot_expression\nlex_time: 1.04880333, parse_time: 1.40404701ms, search_time: 0.00286102ms description: Basic minimal case name: double_dot_expression\nlex_time: 1.05977058, parse_time: 1.42598152ms, search_time: 0.00405312ms description: Basic minimal case name: dot_no_match\nlex_time: 1.11579895, parse_time: 1.56807899ms, search_time: 0.00977516ms description: Deeply nested dict name: deep_nesting_10\nlex_time: 1.52611732, parse_time: 2.52008438ms, search_time: 0.05292892ms description: Deeply nested dict name: deep_nesting_50\nlex_time: 1.07789040, parse_time: 1.47080421ms, search_time: 0.01692772ms description: Multiple wildcards in an expression name: multi_wildcard_field\nlex_time: 1.08003616, parse_time: 1.47199631ms, search_time: 0.01382828ms description: Multiple wildcards in an expression name: wildcard_with_index\nlex_time: 1.05094910, parse_time: 1.41000748ms, search_time: 0.00691414ms description: Multiple wildcards name: wildcard_with_field_match\nlex_time: 1.04999542, parse_time: 1.41096115ms, search_time: 0.00500679ms description: Multiple wildcards name: wildcard_with_field_match2\n```\n\nHand written lexer and parser:\n\n```\n$ python perf/perftest.py\nlex_time: 0.00882149, parse_time: 0.01788139ms, search_time: 0.00190735ms description: Basic minimal case name: single_expression\nlex_time: 0.02598763, parse_time: 0.04196167ms, search_time: 0.00286102ms description: Basic minimal case name: single_dot_expression\nlex_time: 0.02384186, parse_time: 0.04792213ms, search_time: 0.00286102ms description: Basic minimal case name: double_dot_expression\nlex_time: 0.03099442, parse_time: 0.06079674ms, search_time: 0.00500679ms description: Basic minimal case name: dot_no_match\nlex_time: 0.07581711, parse_time: 0.14400482ms, search_time: 0.01287460ms description: Deeply nested dict name: deep_nesting_10\nlex_time: 0.37312508, parse_time: 0.69999695ms, search_time: 0.06580353ms description: Deeply nested dict name: deep_nesting_50\nlex_time: 0.04816055, parse_time: 0.08702278ms, search_time: 0.01192093ms description: Multiple wildcards in an expression name: multi_wildcard_field\nlex_time: 0.04887581, parse_time: 0.08988380ms, search_time: 0.01192093ms description: Multiple wildcards in an expression name: wildcard_with_index\nlex_time: 0.02884865, parse_time: 0.05292892ms, search_time: 0.00691414ms description: Multiple wildcards name: wildcard_with_field_match\nlex_time: 0.02789497, parse_time: 0.05388260ms, search_time: 0.00691414ms description: Multiple wildcards name: wildcard_with_field_match2\n```" <https://repolex.ai/data/jmespath/jmespath.py/commit> .
